WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.740
Welcome to Module 14 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.288
workload identity across
AWS GovCloud and O C I.

3
00:00:08.288 --> 00:00:12.878
It teaches the implementation guide's
steps for the program's other two clouds,

4
00:00:12.878 --> 00:00:15.376
with Chapter 13 of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:15.376 --> 00:00:18.787
The program's AWS estate
is in AWS GovCloud,

6
00:00:18.787 --> 00:00:24.637
and its Oracle Cloud tenancy is in
Oracle's US Government Cloud, realm O C 2.

7
00:00:24.637 --> 00:00:29.962
In November 2026 the program added both
clouds' workload identities to this track.

8
00:00:29.962 --> 00:00:33.160
Take this module after Modules 1 and 4.

9
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.297
Why does this matter?

10
00:00:38.297 --> 00:00:43.109
A workload in AWS or O C I that holds an
access key is a service account with a

11
00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:45.021
password that never expires,

12
00:00:45.021 --> 00:00:48.847
sitting in a console nobody
in identity governance opens.

13
00:00:48.847 --> 00:00:53.198
This module brings those identities under
the same rules as the rest of the track.

14
00:00:53.198 --> 00:00:57.422
Each one is listed and joined to a
record with two owners and a sponsor.

15
00:00:57.422 --> 00:01:01.247
Every long-lived credential is an
exception with a replace-by date.

16
00:01:01.247 --> 00:01:04.385
And every trust is no wider
than its workload needs.

17
00:01:04.385 --> 00:01:07.593
The program decided to extend
the track to both clouds,

18
00:01:07.593 --> 00:01:11.190
and named the tags that join
each identity to its record.

19
00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:17.740
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

20
00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:20.052
which takes about sixty
minutes with the lab.

21
00:01:20.052 --> 00:01:24.359
You'll say why an access key, an
API key, an auth token or a customer

22
00:01:24.359 --> 00:01:29.302
secret key is a long-lived secret, and
what the program does with each one.

23
00:01:29.302 --> 00:01:32.039
You'll name what the
guide's export steps read,

24
00:01:32.039 --> 00:01:35.515
and what AWS's credential report
and list calls leave out.

25
00:01:35.515 --> 00:01:38.577
You'll join an identity to
its record with four tags.

26
00:01:38.577 --> 00:01:42.690
You'll run Chapter 13's checks on
fixtures and read what they find.

27
00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:46.603
And you'll name what's still unknown,
and why the kit doesn't assume an answer.

28
00:01:46.603 --> 00:01:49.689
Before you start, finish Modules 1 and 4.

29
00:01:49.689 --> 00:01:53.624
You need PowerShell 7.1
or later with Pester 5,

30
00:01:53.624 --> 00:01:57.980
and no AWS account, O C I
tenancy or Entra tenant.

31
00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:03.768
Unit 1 is what the vendors say.

32
00:02:03.768 --> 00:02:08.405
AWS says to require workloads to use
temporary credentials with IAM roles,

33
00:02:08.405 --> 00:02:11.580
and where possible not to
create access keys at all.

34
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.264
Oracle recommends instance principals,

35
00:02:14.264 --> 00:02:17.343
and rotating API keys
every ninety days or less.

36
00:02:17.343 --> 00:02:19.742
AWS GovCloud isn't commercial AWS:

37
00:02:19.742 --> 00:02:24.800
it has one IAM control plane and no
global S T S endpoint, no role between a

38
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.030
GovCloud account and a standard one,
and a root user with no console sign-in.

39
00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:34.235
IAM Roles Anywhere works there,
but it can't fetch revocation

40
00:02:34.235 --> 00:02:38.692
lists from a certificate, so AWS
says to import your C R Ls instead.

41
00:02:38.692 --> 00:02:43.034
In O C I, instance and resource
principals act through a dynamic group,

42
00:02:43.034 --> 00:02:46.081
which has no permissions
until a policy names it.

43
00:02:46.081 --> 00:02:49.430
Auth tokens and customer
secret keys never expire.

44
00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:54.138
And Microsoft Learn says Entra
accepts tokens from workloads in AWS,

45
00:02:54.138 --> 00:02:56.368
signed with R S two fifty-six only.

46
00:02:56.368 --> 00:03:00.517
Whether a GovCloud account's tokens
work with Entra hasn't been tested,

47
00:03:00.517 --> 00:03:03.093
so the guide uses that
path only after a test.

48
00:03:03.093 --> 00:03:04.618
The program's rule follows:

49
00:03:04.618 --> 00:03:08.870
every long-lived credential is an
exception with a replacement date.

50
00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:15.032
Unit 2 is the exports and the join.

51
00:03:15.032 --> 00:03:18.233
Every command in the guide's
two export steps is a read.

52
00:03:18.233 --> 00:03:21.975
In AWS, the credential
report lists every IAM user,

53
00:03:21.975 --> 00:03:25.170
its password and its
first two access keys.

54
00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:29.720
It leaves out service-specific credentials
and any key beyond the first two,

55
00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:33.982
so the export also lists access keys
and service-specific credentials.

56
00:03:33.982 --> 00:03:38.432
Then the roles, with their trust
policies, the instance profiles,

57
00:03:38.432 --> 00:03:43.637
the O I D C providers, and the Roles
Anywhere trust anchors, profiles and C R Ls.

58
00:03:43.637 --> 00:03:46.575
List users and list
roles don't return tags,

59
00:03:46.575 --> 00:03:49.933
so the export reads each
user's and role's tags too.

60
00:03:49.933 --> 00:03:53.719
In O C I, against the tenancy's
home region, it reads the users,

61
00:03:53.719 --> 00:03:57.348
each user's credentials, which
come back as identifiers,

62
00:03:57.348 --> 00:04:00.819
never the secret, the dynamic
groups and the policies.

63
00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:06.432
The join is four tags: system I D,
owner one, owner two and sponsor.

64
00:04:06.432 --> 00:04:08.288
Each owner gets its own key,

65
00:04:08.288 --> 00:04:12.169
because an AWS tag value can't
hold a comma or a semicolon.

66
00:04:12.169 --> 00:04:18.220
An identity with no tags or record is an
orphan: disable first, then a person acts.

67
00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:23.845
Unit 3 is the checks.

68
00:04:23.845 --> 00:04:27.535
N P I A W S oh one to oh seven
are the long-lived credentials:

69
00:04:27.535 --> 00:04:32.983
every active access key and service-specific
credential against its exception,

70
00:04:32.983 --> 00:04:36.155
two keys on one user, a
key unused for ninety days,

71
00:04:36.155 --> 00:04:39.082
which is design guidance,
and a root user key.

72
00:04:39.082 --> 00:04:42.682
Oh eight to sixteen are owners
and trust: missing tags,

73
00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:47.262
an O I D C trust with no subject
condition, an outside account, a star or

74
00:04:47.262 --> 00:04:52.333
another partition as the principal, and
a trust anchor with no imported C R L.

75
00:04:52.333 --> 00:04:54.951
N P I O C I oh one to ten
do the same for O C I,

76
00:04:54.951 --> 00:05:00.608
and add dynamic groups that match the
whole tenancy or hold manage all-resources.

77
00:05:00.608 --> 00:05:03.495
The validation kit's V
K N P I ten reads the

78
00:05:03.495 --> 00:05:06.733
same exports and reports
the gate's criteria.

79
00:05:06.733 --> 00:05:11.055
And four things are still unknown,
so the kit doesn't assume an answer:

80
00:05:11.055 --> 00:05:14.375
whether GovCloud and Entra
can federate either way,

81
00:05:14.375 --> 00:05:18.621
whether Access Analyzer's
unused-access findings run in GovCloud,

82
00:05:18.621 --> 00:05:22.944
whether O C 2 offers instance, resource
and O K E workload principals,

83
00:05:22.944 --> 00:05:26.350
and what field names the
O C I command line prints.

84
00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:31.037
Try it.

85
00:05:31.037 --> 00:05:35.926
Lab 1 runs in the non-person identity
folder, with no account and no tenancy.

86
00:05:35.926 --> 00:05:38.637
These are Chapter 13's Lab 1 steps.

87
00:05:38.637 --> 00:05:43.913
The fixtures are one fictional GovCloud
account and one fictional O C 2 tenancy.

88
00:05:43.913 --> 00:05:50.027
Step one runs the checks as of November
24, 2026: seven IAM users, eleven roles

89
00:05:50.027 --> 00:05:55.746
and two trust anchors in AWS; five
users and three dynamic groups in O C I.

90
00:05:55.746 --> 00:05:57.225
Look for two lines.

91
00:05:57.225 --> 00:06:01.138
Twenty-four findings, eleven
warnings and two information items.

92
00:06:01.138 --> 00:06:04.661
And seven AWS and five O C
I long-lived credentials,

93
00:06:04.661 --> 00:06:07.325
two of them under a current exception.

94
00:06:07.325 --> 00:06:10.037
Then one line per rule,
and the manifest's hash.

95
00:06:10.037 --> 00:06:14.231
The script reads files and
writes its output folder:

96
00:06:14.231 --> 00:06:19.212
it runs no AWS or O C I command,
calls no A P I, and changes no key.

97
00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:24.960
Step two reads the findings.

98
00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.935
Find S V C dash report dash export.

99
00:06:27.935 --> 00:06:32.687
It trips four rules: oh one, an
active key with no exception, twice,

100
00:06:32.687 --> 00:06:38.078
once for each key; oh two, two active
keys on one user, one of them never used;

101
00:06:38.078 --> 00:06:43.835
oh four, a console password beside the
keys; and oh eight, only one owner tagged.

102
00:06:43.835 --> 00:06:47.935
Then find D G dash everything,
a dynamic group in O C I.

103
00:06:47.935 --> 00:06:51.359
It has no tags, no policy
names it, and its matching rule

104
00:06:51.359 --> 00:06:55.172
names the whole tenancy, so
every instance would be a member.

105
00:06:55.172 --> 00:06:59.340
Notice the first line: S V C dash
app dash sync's key is a warning,

106
00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:03.240
not a finding, because it's
under a current exception.

107
00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:12.254
Step three reads the long-lived
credentials: one row per active access key,

108
00:07:12.254 --> 00:07:17.382
service-specific credential, API key,
auth token and customer secret key,

109
00:07:17.382 --> 00:07:21.228
with its age, its last use for
AWS keys, and its exception.

110
00:07:21.228 --> 00:07:24.216
Two are under exceptions until 2027:

111
00:07:24.216 --> 00:07:30.291
S V C dash app dash sync's access key and
H R dash report dash reader's API key.

112
00:07:30.291 --> 00:07:31.677
Look at the ages.

113
00:07:31.677 --> 00:07:36.514
S V C dash legacy dash F T P's key
is more than nine hundred days old,

114
00:07:36.514 --> 00:07:41.815
and was last used in December 2025, so
it's also a ninety-day unused lead.

115
00:07:41.815 --> 00:07:45.107
H R dash build dash push's
auth token is five hundred

116
00:07:45.107 --> 00:07:48.477
and eighty-two days old, and
auth tokens never expire.

117
00:07:48.477 --> 00:07:52.290
The file holds identifiers
only, never a secret.

118
00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:58.728
Step four reads who
isn't joined to a record.

119
00:07:58.728 --> 00:08:04.115
Seven identities: two IAM users holding
keys, a Lambda execution role, a role

120
00:08:04.115 --> 00:08:09.553
anyone can assume, two O C I users holding
credentials, and the dynamic group.

121
00:08:09.553 --> 00:08:12.465
Each is an orphan lead under
the guide's orphan rule.

122
00:08:12.465 --> 00:08:15.913
Then it reads one trust: C
I dash pipeline dash O I D C

123
00:08:15.913 --> 00:08:20.202
trusts GitHub's token issuer
with only an audience condition.

124
00:08:20.202 --> 00:08:24.827
AWS's examples limit the token's
subject claim to the one repository.

125
00:08:24.827 --> 00:08:29.702
Without it, workflows from repositories
outside your control could assume the role.

126
00:08:29.702 --> 00:08:32.650
That's N P I A W S oh nine.

127
00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:40.059
Step five runs on an export
made before the guide's export

128
00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:43.437
step added the tag and C R L
reads, so it doesn't have them.

129
00:08:43.437 --> 00:08:46.362
The kit still runs, and says
what it couldn't check.

130
00:08:46.362 --> 00:08:49.412
The manifest's not-read
line names both files.

131
00:08:49.412 --> 00:08:53.432
N P I A W S oh eight becomes one
warning for the whole account,

132
00:08:53.432 --> 00:08:56.112
instead of a finding for each identity.

133
00:08:56.112 --> 00:08:58.770
And oh fourteen warns
for each trust anchor,

134
00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:01.649
because it can't tell
whether a C R L was imported.

135
00:09:01.649 --> 00:09:04.538
A missing file is a warning, never a pass.

136
00:09:04.538 --> 00:09:08.718
Notice too that S V C dash app dash
sync's key is back to a finding,

137
00:09:08.718 --> 00:09:12.051
because this run was
given no exception list.

138
00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:19.311
Step six reads the evidence:
one row per step and control,

139
00:09:19.311 --> 00:09:23.600
with the K S Is aligned with it and
the file that provides the evidence.

140
00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:29.638
The inventory rows are A C two, C M eight
and I A four, from the identities file.

141
00:09:29.638 --> 00:09:32.025
The credentials row is I A five.

142
00:09:32.025 --> 00:09:36.925
The certificates rows are I A five
enhancement two and S C seventeen,

143
00:09:36.925 --> 00:09:40.863
from the trust file, as is A
C six for trust and privilege.

144
00:09:40.863 --> 00:09:45.376
And the lifecycle row is A C two
enhancement three, from the findings.

145
00:09:45.376 --> 00:09:49.000
Match each row to the requirement
table in the module's introduction.

146
00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:52.112
Step seven runs the
tests: thirty-four pass.

147
00:09:52.112 --> 00:09:55.310
Step eight cleans up by
removing the output folder.

148
00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:03.272
Lab 2 runs the same checks on a real
account's and tenancy's exports.

149
00:10:03.272 --> 00:10:06.710
Nobody has run it yet, so no
step's output has been seen.

150
00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:11.841
Use one AWS GovCloud account and one O
C I tenancy that aren't production, with

151
00:10:11.841 --> 00:10:16.972
read access to IAM and Roles Anywhere in
the account, and to IAM in the tenancy.

152
00:10:16.972 --> 00:10:21.860
Write the settings and an empty exception
list, in a folder outside the repository.

153
00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:26.346
Run the guide's AWS export commands,
the tag and C R L reads included,

154
00:10:26.346 --> 00:10:29.835
then the O C I ones against
the tenancy's home region.

155
00:10:29.835 --> 00:10:34.508
Then run the checks, and read the findings
and the manifest: it records no request,

156
00:10:34.508 --> 00:10:38.685
because the kit called nothing, and
lists the fourteen export commands.

157
00:10:38.685 --> 00:10:42.617
If every O C I user shows no credentials
while the console shows keys,

158
00:10:42.617 --> 00:10:46.198
the command line's field names
differ from those the kit reads.

159
00:10:46.198 --> 00:10:47.561
Record what they are.

160
00:10:47.561 --> 00:10:51.630
Keep the exports with the run's
evidence, never in the repository.

161
00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:57.030
The federal requirement.

162
00:10:57.030 --> 00:11:00.443
Chapter 13 writes one evidence
row per step and control,

163
00:11:00.443 --> 00:11:03.455
and these are the rows this
module's lab produces.

164
00:11:03.455 --> 00:11:07.902
The identities file supports A C
two, Account Management, C M eight,

165
00:11:07.902 --> 00:11:16.055
System Component Inventory, and I A four,
Identifier Management, in NIST SP 800-53.

166
00:11:16.055 --> 00:11:18.389
The credentials file supports I A five,

167
00:11:18.389 --> 00:11:22.255
Authenticator Management, with
the exception records it names.

168
00:11:22.255 --> 00:11:25.721
The trust anchors and their
C R Ls support I A five

169
00:11:25.721 --> 00:11:29.880
enhancement two and S C seventeen,
the public key controls.

170
00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:33.918
The trust and reach of every role
and dynamic group support A C six,

171
00:11:33.918 --> 00:11:38.255
Least Privilege, with the owner's
justification for each finding kept.

172
00:11:38.255 --> 00:11:41.539
And the orphan leads support
A C two enhancement three,

173
00:11:41.539 --> 00:11:45.042
Disable Accounts, with the
orphan or decommission ticket.

174
00:11:45.042 --> 00:11:49.667
K S Is are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers.

175
00:11:49.667 --> 00:11:53.780
A row provides evidence for a
control; the assessor decides.

176
00:11:57.580 --> 00:11:59.630
Check yourself with two questions.

177
00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:03.330
First: how does the program
join an IAM role to its record?

178
00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:08.597
The answer is b: four tags, system I
D, owner one, owner two and sponsor,

179
00:12:08.597 --> 00:12:13.668
with one key per owner, because an
AWS tag value can't hold a comma.

180
00:12:13.668 --> 00:12:17.405
Second: the AWS export has no tags file.

181
00:12:17.405 --> 00:12:18.680
What does the kit do?

182
00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:22.037
The answer is b: it runs,
lists the file as not read,

183
00:12:22.037 --> 00:12:24.792
and raises one warning for the account.

184
00:12:24.792 --> 00:12:30.317
A missing file is a warning, never a
pass, and the kit never calls AWS itself.

185
00:12:30.317 --> 00:12:32.436
The module page has three more questions,

186
00:12:32.436 --> 00:12:34.930
each linked to the
section that answers it.

187
00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:39.755
To sum up.

188
00:12:39.755 --> 00:12:45.492
The program's AWS GovCloud and O C I workload
identities follow the track's rules.

189
00:12:45.492 --> 00:12:50.242
Every one is listed from read-only exports
and joined to a record by four tags.

190
00:12:50.242 --> 00:12:53.243
Its long-lived credentials
are exceptions with a date.

191
00:12:53.243 --> 00:12:55.531
Its trusts are named down to the workload.

192
00:12:55.531 --> 00:12:58.793
And its unknowns stay unknowns
until the program tests them.

193
00:12:58.793 --> 00:13:03.256
Chapter 13 checks the exports on
every run, V K N P I ten reports the

194
00:13:03.256 --> 00:13:08.055
gate's criteria, and the evidence
names the control each row supports.

195
00:13:08.055 --> 00:13:11.094
Next, go back to the
learning path's index,

196
00:13:11.094 --> 00:13:14.320
or on to Chapter 13 for
Lab 2 and its dangers.
